course: dictionary
lesson: 10017
title: Context clues: tempura
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: You do not need to know every word to understand a text. Look at the words around tempura for clues. «Japanese tempura grew from Portuguese cooking in the 1500s» tells you tempura is a kind of cooking from Japan.
explain: Other words in the text give more clues too: fried, cooking, sushi and ramen are all foods, so tempura must be a food. Check your meaning by swapping it into the sentence. "Japanese food grew from Portuguese cooking in the 1500s" still makes sense, so you are on the right track.
explain: Tempura is a Japanese dish of pieces of seafood, like prawns, or vegetables. They are dipped in a light batter and deep-fried until crispy. It is usually eaten with a dipping sauce.
fact: Tempura is an uncountable noun, so you say some tempura, not a tempura.
fact: Tempura is a Japanese dish of seafood or vegetables in a light batter, deep-fried until crispy.
fact: Batter is a runny mixture, a bit like pancake mix, that coats the food before frying.
fact: Tempura is usually eaten with a dipping sauce.
fact: Deep-frying uses very hot oil, so only adults should do it.
exercise: «Japanese tempura grew from Portuguese cooking in the 1500s.» Which country's food is tempura? | *Japan | Portugal | Spain | Britain
exercise: Prawn tempura is a phrase you may see in a food lesson. What does it mean? | *Prawns dipped in light batter and deep-fried until crispy | Prawns baked in a sweet sauce | Prawns boiled in plain water and served cold | Prawns frozen with ice and eaten raw
exercise: Why should only adults deep-fry tempura? | Children cannot hold a fork | Tempura is too sweet to cook | Adults are better at dipping sauce | *Deep-frying uses very hot oil
test: What is tempura usually eaten with? | *A dipping sauce | Milk | Sugar sprinkled on it | Ketchup poured on the top only
test: Which meaning fits best for vegetable tempura? | *Vegetables dipped in light batter and deep-fried | Vegetables eaten raw in a salad | Vegetables frozen with ice | Vegetables boiled in plain water
exam: Sofia says tempura is a kind of sushi. What is the right answer? | Yes, sushi is always fried in batter | *No, tempura is seafood or vegetables in batter, deep-fried | No, tempura is a drink | Yes, both are raw fish
